WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
Least privilege turns sandboxing from a process layout into an enforceable policy.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:12.440
Start with the smallest set of permissions the component needs, then add access only when a feature has a documented requirement.

3
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:20.220
Each permission should have an owner, a reason, and tests that show what happens when access is allowed or denied.

4
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:27.520
A permission request that cannot be explained should not be treated as routine plumbing during formal code review.

5
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.120
Operating systems provide different sandbox and containment primitives.

6
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:50.140
Depending on platform and product requirements, teams may use reduced permissions, restricted tokens, user or session separation, application containers, entitlements, sandbox profiles, job controls, namespaces, syscall filtering concepts, or managed platform frameworks.

7
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:56.160
The details vary, so C++ applications should follow approved platform guidance and organization policy.

8
00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:03.780
Treat those mechanisms as policy tools, not decorative settings: they should express the exact resource model the component is supposed to have.

9
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.460
Deny-by-default thinking is useful even when the platform primitives differ.

10
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:17.120
The worker should not receive broad filesystem, network, device, UI, environment, or service access just because the full application has it.

11
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:23.060
Permissions should be added for the worker's job, not inherited from the most capable component in the product.

12
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.300
Policy review should happen whenever features change.

13
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:34.960
A new import format, plugin model, device integration, update path, or telemetry feature may require new access.

14
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.420
That does not make the access wrong, but it should be explicit.

15
00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:45.880
A sandbox policy that nobody owns tends to grow quietly until it no longer expresses the original boundary.

16
00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:52.140
Version policy changes with the feature, and keep tests for both allowed and denied access.

17
00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:58.000
This gives reviewers a way to distinguish intentional capability from accidental permission growth.
